<!-- Saint James (Son of Alphaeus)
James (Ἰάκωβος) bar Alphaeus: "James the Less"

(Parallel to "Simon bar Jonah: A Galilean Fisherman")

Name, Identity, and Social Position
Hebrew name "Jacob" (Ἰάκωβος), rendered "James" in English — the same underlying name as James son of Zebedee, requiring careful disambiguation from the outset
Identified as "son of Alphaeus" (Matthew 10:3; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:15; Acts 1:13) — directly raising the shared-name puzzle flagged on Matthew's page, since Matthew/Levi is also called "son of Alphaeus" (Mark 2:14)
Most interpreters treat Matthew and James as two separate men with fathers who happened to share the common name Alphaeus, rather than as brothers — but Scripture never states this explicitly, so the ambiguity should be presented as open rather than resolved
Traditionally called "James the Less" or "James the Younger" (ὁ μικρός, Mark 15:40) — likely distinguishing him by age, stature, or lesser prominence from James son of Zebedee, rather than indicating any lesser status in the apostolic band itself
Sometimes conflated in later tradition with "James the brother of the Lord" (Galatians 1:19) and with the author of the Epistle of James — a identification followed in some Catholic tradition (linked to a proposed identity as "James son of Alphaeus/Clopas") but rejected by many Protestant and critical scholars, who treat these as two or three distinct figures
This layered identity question is worth stating as the single most complex disambiguation problem in the series so far — more tangled than Bartholomew/Nathanael or Matthew/Levi, since it involves potentially three overlapping "James" figures across the New Testament rather than two names for one man
Family Structure & Responsibilities
Father named as Alphaeus, paralleling the same name given for Matthew's father — no further confirmed relationship established between the two men in Scripture
Some traditions identify his mother as "Mary the mother of James" present at the crucifixion and resurrection (Matthew 27:56; Mark 15:40; Luke 24:10) — if correct, this places his mother among the women who witnessed both events directly, though the identification is not certain
If Alphaeus is identified with "Clopas" (John 19:25) in some traditions, this would make James a relative of Jesus through Mary's extended family — a connection presented in some strands of tradition but far from settled
Social and Cultural Environment
No specific hometown or trade recorded in Scripture, continuing the pattern of biographical silence seen with Thomas and, to a lesser extent, Philip and Bartholomew
Religious Life
No individual statement, question, or action attributed to him anywhere in the four Gospels — a distinction unique even within this already-quiet cluster of apostles, since Philip, Bartholomew, and Thomas each have at least one substantial personal episode
No basis in Scripture for reconstructing his personal faith or spiritual disposition prior to or during his discipleship
Political Awareness
No information preserved in Scripture
Reputation in the Community
Entirely undocumented outside his inclusion in the Twelve — he holds the distinction of being the least individually attested member of the apostolic band in the canonical Gospels and Acts alike
James the Called

(Parallel to "The Day the Nets Were Full" and the calling sections of the other apostles)

No individual calling narrative recorded in Scripture, matching the gap already noted on Thomas's page
First appears simply as a name within the formal appointment of the Twelve (Mark 3:13-19; Luke 6:12-16), listed as "James the son of Alphaeus" in all four enumerations
Worth stating plainly: James son of Alphaeus is the first apostle in this series with neither a calling narrative nor any individual episode anywhere in the Gospels — a stronger silence even than Thomas, who at least has no calling story but does have substantial later material
James's Journey

(Parallel to "Cephas's Journey": Walking on Water, Transfiguration, Denial/Restoration)

A Complete Absence of Individual Narrative
No episode, question, statement, or action is attributed to James son of Alphaeus individually anywhere in the four Gospels
His entire scriptural presence consists of his name appearing in the four lists of the Twelve (Matthew 10:3; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:15; Acts 1:13)
This should be stated directly and without embellishment, continuing the honest approach to biographical gaps established on Philip's, Bartholomew's, and Thomas's pages — there is no episode to reconstruct or dramatize here, only the fact of his consistent inclusion among the Twelve
James the Apostle of the Early Church

(Parallel to Peter's leadership: Pentecost, Miracles, Imprisonment, Council)

Holy Spirit Praxis
Listed among the Twelve gathered in the upper room after the Ascension (Acts 1:13)
Present at Pentecost by inclusion in "the Twelve," though no individual action is attributed to him anywhere in Acts, continuing the pattern of Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, and Matthew
Absence from Individual Narrative in Acts
James son of Alphaeus recedes from the biblical narrative entirely after Acts 1:13, making this the fifth consecutive apostle in your series with this exact shape
Unlike Matthew, he leaves behind no compensating literary legacy — no Gospel, no epistle confidently attributed to him under this identification
The Council of Jerusalem
No individual role attributed to him in Acts 15
Presumed presence among "the apostles and elders" as a member of the Twelve, though not named specifically
Worth noting explicitly: if this James were the same figure as "James the brother of the Lord," he would have played a central role in the Council (Acts 15:13-21) — but this is precisely the identification most scholars reject, so that prominent Council role belongs on a different figure's page, not confidently on this one
James's Later Ministry

(A distinctive tradition-dependent section, parallel to Matthew's and Thomas's "Later Ministry" sections)

Early Christian tradition is thin and inconsistent compared to even Matthew's divided accounts
Some traditions associate him with missionary work in Syria; others, reflecting the conflation with James the brother of the Lord, transfer that figure's well-documented Jerusalem ministry onto him — a conflation this outline should flag rather than adopt
Western tradition (reflected in some medieval sources) links him loosely to Persia, sometimes in company with the apostle Jude/Thaddaeus, though this tradition is notably weaker and later than the Thomas-in-India or Bartholomew-in-Armenia traditions
This outline should treat all such claims with more caution than usual, given how easily traditions about this James have blended with traditions about other, better-attested figures of the same name
James's Writings

(Parallel to "Matthew's Writings" / "Thomas's Writings")

No writing is confidently attributed to James son of Alphaeus under critical consensus
The canonical Epistle of James is traditionally attributed to "James the brother of the Lord" (or, in some traditions, conflated with this James) rather than confidently to James son of Alphaeus specifically
This outline should treat the Epistle of James as belonging properly to a separate figure's page (James the brother of Jesus) rather than claiming it here, even though some older devotional traditions have made that link
No apocryphal literature of note is attached specifically to this James, unlike the apocryphal texts attributed to Thomas, Bartholomew, or Philip
The Final Journey

(Parallel to the Final Journey sections of all preceding apostles)

What the Bible Tells Us
Scripture gives no account whatsoever of his later life, ministry, or death — his last scriptural appearance is his listing in Acts 1:13, identical in shape to the silences noted for Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, and Matthew, but total from the very beginning rather than following any recorded personal episode
James's Ministry and Death (Early Christian Tradition)
Tradition is genuinely unsettled, more so than for any other apostle in this series
Some traditions describe martyrdom by stoning; others by crucifixion; the location shifts between Jerusalem, Syria, Egypt, and Persia depending on the source and on which conflated "James" tradition is being followed
This outline should present this section as the least reliable "Final Journey" of the series, since much of the source material likely describes James the brother of the Lord rather than James son of Alphaeus, and disentangling the two with confidence is not possible from the available sources
James's Martyrdom
No single tradition rises to clear prominence in the way Thomas's spearing or Bartholomew's flaying do
Best practice for this outline is to state plainly that reliable information is not available, rather than presenting any single account as authoritative
James's Legacy

(Parallel to the Legacy sections of all preceding apostles)

Patron saint associations exist (including, in some traditions, of the dying and of pharmacists) but rest on thin and contested historical ground
His enduring significance lies less in personal legacy and more in what his obscurity demonstrates about the apostolic band as a whole: not every member of the Twelve left a developed narrative, a writing, or even a single recorded word, and Scripture is comfortable naming such figures without further embellishment
A useful closing point for your series: James son of Alphaeus functions almost as a structural counterweight to Peter, Paul, and John — a reminder that apostolic authority and inclusion in Christ's inner circle did not depend on individual prominence, eloquence, or a developed personal narrative
Feast days observed separately in Eastern and Western traditions, often complicated by the same identification questions that affect the rest of his page -->